I said, "spoilers".



(1) It has been reported that an elven bow does 1-35 points of damage
against small monsters.  Since there is no #fire command, Bonga the
Genin was a bit suspicious, so he checked this report out with his
stethescope.  While wielding a +2 elven bow, he threw some elven
arrows.  Then he unwielded the bow, and threw some more.  On average
the arrows did 5 HP each of damage to small monsters, whether the bow
was wielded or not.  Not very impressive.  Then he tried throwing the
bow at a xan.  Splat!  It really does 1-35 points of damage against
small monsters.  Unfortunately, it only does 1-6 points of damage
against large monsters.

Here is a list of small monsters: acid blob, bat, brown mold, cave
spider, chameleon, cockatrice, fire ant, floating eye, freezing
sphere, garter snake, gecko, giant ant, giant rat, gnome, gnome king,
gnome lord, gnome mummy, gnome zombie, gnomish wizard, goblin, green
mold, gremlin, grid bug, hell hound pup, hobbit, homunculus, housecat,
iguana, imp, jackal, jackalwere, killer bee, kitten, kobold, kobold
lord, kobold mummy, kobold shaman, kobold zombie, large cat, large
kobold, leprechaun, little dog, lizard, manes, newt, quasit, queen
bee, quivering blob, rabid rat, ratwere, red mold, rock mole, rock
piercer, scorpion, sewer rat, snake, soldier ant, tengu, vampire bat,
violet fungus, water moccasin, wolf, xan, yellow light, yellow mold.

Note: crossbows do 1-35, plain bows do 1-30, and orcish bows do 1-25.
You might prefer a crossbow, because they are cheaper.

(2) Bonga observed that his pet cat named Baby gains at least one MAX
HP each time she kills a monster --- more if the monster is tough.
Pretty soon Baby was up to 96(302) HP.  Then she killed a newt, and
her hitpoints were 72(303), even though the newt never hit her.
Apparently the code limits a cat's hit points to 8 times her level
(the max level for a cat is 9), but this portion of the code is only
invoked when the cat kills something, so while resting she can regain
up to 300 hit points.

(3) A number of people have asked about fighting in their sleep (when
fainting from hunger).  All sleep-resisant characters are able to do
this.  Some characters like elves start with sleep resistance.  Others
gain it via proper diet.  The failure to faint is not a bug.  However,
you might say that the status line is misleading.  I'd write
"starving" instead of "fainting" in the corner of the screen.

